add('Grammar Discovery','Noticing',2,section('Read first, notice second',prompt('Look at these examples from the article. What changes when we compare two things or a whole group?')+`<div class="soft"><p>1. The game is <b>way better than</b> the mobile version.</p><p>2. This fanfic is <b>more exciting than</b> the last one.</p><p>3. Watching a video isn’t <b>quite as exciting as</b> being there.</p><p>4. This is <b>the most underrated</b> hobby.</p><p>5. It’s <b>by far the best</b> performance.</p></div>`+radio('notice1','Which examples compare two things directly?',['1, 2 and 3','4 and 5','Only 5'],0)+radio('notice2','Which expression makes the difference sound much bigger?',['quite as','way','the'],1)+checkbar())+section('Think about it',`<p class="muted">Why is <b>the best</b> different from <b>better</b>? When could you say <b>not nearly as good as</b>?</p>`),'Grammar');
add('Rule 1 · Comparatives','Grammar with practice',3,section('Grammar card 1',`<div class="rule">${ruleTitle(1,'Comparatives: two things')}<p>We use a <b>comparative</b> when we compare two people, things or situations. We often add <b>than</b>.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="table"><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>Short adjective + <b>-er</b></td><td>small → smaller</td></tr><tr><td>Consonant + y → <b>-ier</b></td><td>busy → busier</td></tr><tr><td>Short CVC: double consonant</td><td>big → bigger</td></tr><tr><td>More / less + longer adjective</td><td>more interesting / less exciting</td></tr><tr><td>Irregular adjectives</td><td>good → better; bad → worse</td></tr></table></div><div class="rule-example">“I’m <b>busier</b> this week <b>than</b> last week.”<br>“A cosy evening is <b>more relaxing than</b> a chaotic shopping trip.”</div><p class="micro">Avoid: <i>more better</i>, <i>gooder</i>, <i>more easier</i>.</p></div>`)+qsection('Your turn · Complete 4 sentences',prompt('Use the comparative form of the adjective in brackets.')+
